  "answer": "For an opposition to registration, lodge the following with the Trade Marks Office:\n\n1. **An application for an extension of time**, in an approved form, identifying whether you seek more time to file the notice of intention to oppose or the statement of grounds and particulars.\n2. **The relevant fee.** The Manual says the request will not be processed until it is paid. [TMM/Part47/1/1/3/2]\n3. **A supporting declaration complying with reg 21.6**, explaining the reasons supporting your grounds for an extension. Because the deadline has expired, the Manual says the declaration should also explain why the extension application was not made within the original period. [TMM/Part47/1/1/3/2]\n\n**Manual practice:** IP Australia provides a form on its website, but the Manual says using that particular form is not compulsory. The approved-form requirement still applies. [TMM/Part47/1/1/3/2]\n\nFor a late request, the Manual says the Registrar must be satisfied both about the grounds and supporting reasons, and that there was sufficient reason for not applying within time. This is not a prediction that your request will succeed. [TMM/Part47/1/1/3/3]\n\n**Act:** Section 52(2) recognises filing within an extended opposition period. [TMA1995/s52]\n\n**Limit of the supplied material:** The Regulations themselves are not reproduced here. I cannot give the declaration’s detailed formal requirements, a complete list of allowable extension grounds or the fee amount from these extracts."
